   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What is happening in the body",
   "text": "Rett syndrome is caused by changes, called mutations, in a gene named MECP2 [E43eb216]. These changes stop the gene from working the way it normally should, which scientists call a loss of function [E60b5855]. MECP2 makes a protein that acts a bit like a switch, helping turn other genes up or down inside cells [Ed634190, E075bb94]. In most children with Rett syndrome, about 95 out of 100, this MECP2 change is what is found [Ed634190]. The MECP2 protein normally attaches to DNA and helps control whether other genes are read, a job called transcription [E19525f3, Eb2d00d1]. Because MECP2 is not working properly, many of the genes it controls get out of balance, something called a transcriptional or chromatin regulation defect [Ea777cdb, E6e7e37a, E47608a9]. This same gene problem also affects how brain cells talk to each other at their connection points, called synapses, known as synaptic dysfunction [Ee884043].",
