  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "Research, trials, and groups that exist",
   "text": "Several patient groups focus on Rett syndrome, including the International Rett Syndrome Foundation in the US [E6c11442], Abre-te in Brazil [Ecc0302d], the Ontario Rett Syndrome Association of Canada [E4616ce3], Rett Syndrome Research Trust in the US [E8a6bc91], and Rett UK [Eb84116b]. These groups run registries and studies, such as the Rett Syndrome Global Registry [Ee240eaa], the Rett Syndrome Natural History Study [Ef49c2ad], the Rett Syndrome Registry [Eb3ee9ce], the Rett Patient Registry [Efdddf4b], and the British Isles Rett Syndrome Survey [Ea7da948]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, there is a recruiting phase 1 trial testing vorinostat against placebo for Rett syndrome with a site in Colombia [Ed541c64, E2694c64, E1100a1a]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, there is a recruiting phase 3 trial testing fenfluramine hydrochloride for Rett syndrome with sites in several countries including the United States, Belgium, France, Germany, and Hungary [E79f8289, E6c0022c, E39ff509, E8cebd94, E1080eee, Eed60960]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a trial of NTI164 against placebo for Rett syndrome is not yet recruiting, with a planned site in Australia [E62c0619, E4e74b09, E186ac35]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, a trial of GCB-002 for Rett syndrome is enrolling by invitation, with a site in China [E83324e1, Ebb82434]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, there is also a web-based support study for parents of children with genetic syndromes, active but not recruiting, with a site in the United States [E3fb6961, E3796182]. According to the NIH Reporter database, funders including NIH NINDS, NIH NHLBI, and NIH NICHD support research grants that study Rett syndrome, with named researchers listed as grant leaders [E09218ac, E67a3b37, E9f524a7, E3a592e2, E27ddbe1, Ed8e53b0, E426f86b, Eac63c81, Ea200cdc].",
